---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";
---
<Base title="Roadmap"
      eyebrow="What is left, in the order it is worth doing"
      dek="One question now stands above all the others, and there are seven ways at it. This page ranks them by what they would actually settle, and says plainly which need a person rather than a search.">

<section class="blk">
  <h2>The one question</h2>
  <div class="prose">
    <p><strong>Who were Pablo Armando Lerena's parents?</strong> Everything else in this archive is either
      answered or downstream of that. The line below him is closed and documented; above him there is
      nothing at all.</p>
    <p>And a second question has grown out of the first, because the Argentine record has now refused three
      times: <strong>was he really born at Rosario?</strong></p>
  </div>
  <div class="grid g3" style="margin-top:24px">
    <div class="panel"><span class="chip doc">Refusal 1</span>
      <p class="ab"><strong>Fourteen years of baptisms.</strong> The cathedral index read line by line,
        1882&ndash;1895 — and until 1888 there was no other parish in Rosario.</p></div>
    <div class="panel"><span class="chip doc">Refusal 2</span>
      <p class="ab"><strong>The whole FamilySearch index.</strong> Two records exist for him on earth: a
        Cape marriage and a modern grave memorial.</p></div>
    <div class="panel"><span class="chip doc">Refusal 3</span>
      <p class="ab"><strong>The 1895 national census.</strong> Not one Lerena recorded in Santa Fe.</p></div>
  </div>
  <div class="note" style="margin-top:24px">And there is no civil fallback. <strong>Argentine civil
    registration only began around 1886</strong>, so for a birth in February 1882 the parish register is
    the only record that could ever have existed. This is not a gap in the searching. It is the shape of
    the Argentine record system.</div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>The theory that would explain all three refusals</h2>
  <p><span class="chip inf">Hypothesis</span> <strong>He may not be indexed under Lerena at all.</strong></p>
  <div class="prose" style="margin-top:14px">
    <p>This family's documented practice was to have children first and marry later. <strong>Roque was born
      in November 1905; his parents did not marry until September 1911</strong>, and the register calls
      them bachelor and spinster. The child was baptised that June, the parents married in September, the
      mother was received into the church in December. An entire family regularised inside one year.</p>
    <p>In Spanish parish and civil practice, <strong>a child born outside marriage is commonly registered
      under the mother's surname</strong>. If Pablo Armando was himself born that way — and the habit
      plainly ran in the family — then he was born at Rosario exactly as his passport says, and he is
      simply <strong>not under L</strong>. Note too that his passport carries <em>one</em> surname where
      Spanish practice ordinarily gives two.</p>
    <p class="cap">David's own tree already leans this way: it carries a <strong>Lerena mother</strong> and
      a <strong>Llorens father</strong> for him, on the strength of a Buenos Aires baptism of 1872 that he
      himself marks unproven. The shape is right even if that particular record is not.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>Seven routes, ranked</h2>

  <h3 style="margin-top:24px">1 · His military enrolment <span class="chip doc" style="margin-left:8px">needs an email</span></h3>
  <p>His passport records a <em>libreta de enrolamiento</em> and service in the <strong>Guardia Nacional,
    6º de Caballería, 1915</strong>. Enrolment was compulsory from <strong>Ley 4031 (1901)</strong>, the
    libreta from <strong>Ley 8129 (1911)</strong>; born 1882 makes him <strong>clase 1882</strong>.
    <strong>An Argentine enrolment record names the man's parents and his birthplace.</strong> Checked and
    recorded: FamilySearch's indexed military records return <strong>zero</strong> for this surname, so this
    is not a search — it is a letter to the <strong>Archivo General del Ejército</strong>. One is drafted in
    Spanish and ready to send.</p>

  <h3 style="margin-top:24px">2 · The consulate that issued the passport <span class="chip doc" style="margin-left:8px">needs an email</span></h3>
  <p>To be issued an Argentine passport in 1940 he had to prove nationality, so the Cape Town consulate held
    a <em>matrícula consular</em> on him — and those files went home to the
    <strong>Archivo del Ministerio de Relaciones Exteriores y Culto</strong>. Whatever he produced to prove
    he was Argentine is the document this archive is looking for.</p>

  <h3 style="margin-top:24px">3 · CEMLA — his parents arriving <span class="chip inf" style="margin-left:8px">needs a person</span></h3>
  <p>The index of arrivals into Buenos Aires from 1882. It would find <strong>his parents</strong>, with
    ages, nationality, ship and date. It is free, and it is behind a <strong>CAPTCHA</strong>, which this
    archive's researcher will not complete. Five searches are written out, forename blank, across
    <strong>Lerena · Llerena · Larena · Lerina</strong>. Five minutes' work for a human being.</p>

  <h3 style="margin-top:24px">4 · He came with the horses <span class="chip lore" style="margin-left:8px">untried, and the biggest idea here</span></h3>
  <p>We have been hunting a <strong>passenger</strong>. He may never have been one. The South African War
    shipped some <strong>360,000 horses</strong> into the country, <strong>Argentina among the main
    sources</strong>. He was <strong>seventeen when it began and twenty when it ended</strong>, a horseman
    by trade and a horse-dealer at the Cape by 1911. Horses travel with <strong>conductors, grooms and
    muleteers</strong>, engaged at the port of loading — and those men are written down in
    <strong>military remount paperwork, not civilian passenger lists</strong>. That would explain why he is
    invisible in every passenger index searched.</p>
  <p style="margin-top:12px">Where: <strong>The National Archives, Kew — WO 108</strong> and related Remount
    Department series; the Cape remount depots, chiefly <strong>Stellenbosch</strong>; and the British
    purchasing commissions at Buenos Aires. <strong>None of it has been touched.</strong></p>

  <h3 style="margin-top:24px">5 · Read 1882 across every letter <span class="chip inf" style="margin-left:8px">expensive but finite</span></h3>
  <p>If the theory above is right, he is in the Rosario index under some other surname. The index is
    arranged by letter and then by year, so testing it means reading the <strong>1882 band of every
    letter</strong> for a <em>Pablo Armando</em>. Perhaps forty page-openings. The method is now known and
    the film is open: <span class="mono">DGS 4530325</span>, at fit zoom with region crops.
    Start with <strong>F, for Fernández</strong>, which is the surname David's own hypothesis proposes.</p>

  <h3 style="margin-top:24px">6 · Uruguay <span class="chip inf" style="margin-left:8px">not a fallback</span></h3>
  <p>The world's densest Lerena population; a documented Montevideo kindred in the
    <strong>Lerena Lenguas</strong>; and a household that crossed to Buenos Aires in the late 1880s. On the
    evidence gathered, this is not where you go when Argentina fails — it is where the surname actually
    lives. Montevideo civil registration and parish registers.</p>

  <h3 style="margin-top:24px">7 · The 1935 mission census <span class="chip lore" style="margin-left:8px">a curiosity worth an hour</span></h3>
  <p>Both younger sons appear in an <strong>LDS South African Mission census of 1935</strong>, each indexed
    as <strong>"Son"</strong> — which means a <strong>head of household</strong> stands on that page, and it
    should be Pablo Armando, giving his own birthplace <strong>five years before the passport and
    independent of it</strong>. The image is not available online. Why two Catholic boys of eleven and
    fifteen are in a Mormon mission census in the year their mother died is a question in itself.</p>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>What would close it fastest</h2>
  <div class="prose">
    <p><strong>Routes 1 and 3, this week.</strong> One is an email that costs nothing; the other is five
      minutes at a keyboard. Between them they are the two cheapest shots at his parents, and neither has
      been fired.</p>
    <p>If both come back empty, <strong>route 4</strong> is where the real thinking has to go — because if
      he came to Africa with a cargo of horses rather than a ticket, then no passenger list will ever
      have him, and we have been looking in the wrong archive from the beginning.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="note" style="margin-top:24px">Nothing here unseats the passport. It is his own document,
    sworn at a consulate, naming a city, a province and a day. What the record refuses to do, so far, is
    agree with it — and an archive that says so plainly is worth more than one that quietly stops
    looking.</div>
</section>
</Base>
